WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Perfect forwarding is useful because it preserves value category.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.280
A generic wrapper can pass lvalues as lvalues and rvalues as rvalues.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.260
That is powerful, but it preserves mechanics rather than design intent.

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.760
The caller may not know whether the template will store, borrow, move from, or simply inspect the value.

5
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:27.960
Forwarding references can accidentally accept temporaries that do not live long enough for the template's later behavior.

6
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:36.700
Generic code may store a reference, create a view, capture a callback, or pass an object into another layer that retains it.

7
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:43.120
If lifetime is not documented, the API can look safe while carrying a dangling reference risk.

8
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.640
Ownership should be visible in the API shape.

9
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.700
A template that stores an object should say so and usually take ownership explicitly.

10
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.680
A template that borrows only during the call should avoid storing borrowed state.

11
00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:03.620
A template that transfers ownership should make that transfer visible through the parameter type or documentation.

12
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:14.940
Lifetime-sensitive types deserve extra review: spans, string views, iterators, references, callbacks, coroutine handles, and non-owning wrappers.

13
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.920
They are efficient and useful, but they depend on another object staying alive.

14
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.980
Generic code can hide that dependency if it accepts everything through one broad forwarding path.

15
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.460
Move behavior also needs intent.

16
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:35.120
Moving from a parameter can be correct, but generic code should not surprise callers by consuming an object earlier than expected.

17
00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.700
Defensive templates make borrowing, storing, moving, and viewing part of the contract.

18
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:47.500
Tests should include temporary arguments, lvalue arguments, moved-from objects, and borrowed views when those cases are supported.

19
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:54.160
Those tests document whether the template preserves, consumes, or only observes its inputs.
